WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.097
INSERT adds new rows to a table.

00:00:02.097 --> 00:00:06.137
A good practice is to name the target columns explicitly.

00:00:06.137 --> 00:00:11.622
That makes it clear which value belongs to which field and makes the statement more resilient if the table

00:00:11.622 --> 00:00:11.962
structure later changes.

00:00:11.962 --> 00:00:17.542
PostgreSQL RETURNING can immediately show the row that was stored, including a generated primary key.

00:00:17.542 --> 00:00:24.842
Before running this example, look at the values and make sure the medical record number is unique and the age

00:00:24.842 --> 00:00:29.697
satisfies the table constraint. Then run it and inspect the returned row.

00:00:29.697 --> 00:00:34.267
If you copy the statement into pgAdmin, compare the returned values with a SELECT for the same unique key.

00:00:34.267 --> 00:00:39.312
INSERT changes stored data, so verification is now part of the workflow, not an optional extra step.

00:00:39.312 --> 00:00:43.372
Writes deserve a slower workflow than reads.

00:00:43.372 --> 00:00:47.397
Before UPDATE or DELETE, run the intended WHERE condition as a SELECT and inspect the affected rows.

00:00:47.397 --> 00:00:52.087
In production, use transactions when appropriate so you can verify the change before committing.

00:00:52.087 --> 00:00:57.840
The absence of a syntax error does not mean the correct records were modified.
